+- `cf` signs in with one scoped API token. Its wrapper reads the token from an encrypted secret on every run and passes it, with the token's account, to that `cf` process alone, so `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` is unset in your shell on purpose. Do not run `cf auth login`, `cf auth create`, or `cf auth activate`, and never read, print, or copy the token.
+- If `cf` says it has no token and runs signed out, or `cf auth whoami` reports `"authenticated": false`, this machine has no token yet. Tell the user; do not sign in another way.
+- The token grants only the permissions the user chose. When the API refuses a request for lack of permission, tell the user which permission the command needs; do not look for other credentials.
+- A project that brings its own token, in the environment or in a `.env` file in the current directory, uses that token and its account instead.
+- The token can change real resources. Preview each change with `--dry-run`, and get the user's approval before running a command that creates, changes, or deletes anything.
 
